{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>וְאָכַלְתָּ וְשָׂבָעְתָּ</b> (\"and you shall eat and be satisfied\"). This is yet another blessing — that there be a blessing present in the bread within your innards, so that you eat and are satisfied. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 11:15). And R. Abraham ibn Ezra said (Ibn Ezra on Deuteronomy 11:15) that <b>וְאָכַלְתָּ וְשָׂבָעְתָּ</b> returns to your grain, your wine, and your oil — not to what immediately precedes it, which is <b>עֵשֶׂב בְּשָׂדְךָ</b> (\"grass in your field\"). But the correct interpretation in my eyes is that it returns to everything: you shall eat and be satisfied with grain, wine, and oil, and also the offspring of sheep and cattle. And in the Sifrei (Eikev 15): \"וְאָכַלְתָּ וְשָׂבָעְתָּ — when your livestock eats and is satisfied, the land works well,\" as it says (Proverbs 14:4), \"and abundance of produce comes by the strength of the ox.\" Another interpretation: from the offspring — although there is no proof for the matter, it is a remembrance for the matter, as it says (Jeremiah 31:11), \"and they shall come and sing on the height of Zion, and they shall flow to the goodness of YHWH, over grain and wine and oil, and over the offspring of sheep and cattle,\" etc. And this is the correct interpretation."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:47:56.513108704Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}